WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.560
Thirteen services and the schema loader.

2
00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:05.720
Every source reads azure.

3
00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:08.600
None of these came from
the community registry.

4
00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:13.080
Each one is a build of a
service fork's main branch.

5
00:00:13.790 --> 00:00:15.560
So whose image is running?

6
00:00:15.950 --> 00:00:17.000
Follow one.

7
00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:25.810
Fieldnote eight.

8
00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:27.420
Whose image is running?

9
00:00:28.494 --> 00:00:31.734
Which build does each service
run, where did it come from,

10
00:00:31.734 --> 00:00:33.424
and how long can it stay?

11
00:00:34.990 --> 00:00:38.670
Every service runs an image built
from its Azure fork's main branch.

12
00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:42.840
spi up chooses it once,
the image lock records it,

13
00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:45.880
and it stays until a refresh moves it.

14
00:00:47.646 --> 00:00:53.806
At spi up, the CLI asks GHCR,
for each of thirteen forks,

15
00:00:53.806 --> 00:00:58.856
which digest main-snapshot names, and
records it under the commit's sha tag.

16
00:00:59.486 --> 00:01:02.616
Schema's loader comes from
the same commit as schema.

17
00:01:03.326 --> 00:01:05.976
The community registry is not a source:

18
00:01:06.126 --> 00:01:08.936
its images don't carry the Azure provider.

19
00:01:09.526 --> 00:01:13.256
An environment can record a
trusted mirror for one service.

20
00:01:13.486 --> 00:01:15.016
This one had none.

21
00:01:17.282 --> 00:01:20.412
Partition's tag names a
commit on its fork's main,

22
00:01:20.442 --> 00:01:23.612
an upstream integration
merged that same day.

23
00:01:24.562 --> 00:01:30.242
On GHCR, that tag points at one
digest, and that digest is what both

24
00:01:30.242 --> 00:01:31.932
partition pods run.

25
00:01:32.882 --> 00:01:36.082
A tag can move. The lock keeps the digest,

26
00:01:36.082 --> 00:01:37.772
and the build date beside it.

27
00:01:40.286 --> 00:01:42.536
That image does not move on its own.

28
00:01:42.766 --> 00:01:47.486
Every Monday, each fork prunes
sha images older than thirty days,

29
00:01:47.486 --> 00:01:49.576
once main has built past them.

30
00:01:49.886 --> 00:01:56.136
So spi info and spi status mark a canonical
built more than twenty-one days ago,

31
00:01:56.166 --> 00:01:58.136
which leaves nine days.

32
00:01:58.686 --> 00:02:03.846
On the shared environment, a run every
weekday morning refreshes every service,

33
00:02:03.846 --> 00:02:05.656
an hour ahead of Monday's prune.

34
00:02:06.046 --> 00:02:07.576
On yours, that's you:

35
00:02:07.886 --> 00:02:09.976
spi service refresh.

36
00:02:12.458 --> 00:02:14.228
Follow partition's row home.

37
00:02:14.618 --> 00:02:17.188
spi info reads the image lock.

38
00:02:17.418 --> 00:02:20.788
The lock holds the
repository, the commit's tag,

39
00:02:20.938 --> 00:02:23.108
the digest and the build date.

40
00:02:23.578 --> 00:02:27.178
On GHCR, that tag points
at the same digest.

41
00:02:27.178 --> 00:02:29.418
The tag names a merge on the fork's main.

42
00:02:29.418 --> 00:02:33.268
And spi status shows both
pods running that digest.

43
00:02:33.498 --> 00:02:37.828
Whose image? The fork's, from
one commit you can read.

44
00:02:40.886 --> 00:02:42.216
An easy mistake:

45
00:02:42.366 --> 00:02:45.736
my environment picks up the
fork's new main on its own.

46
00:02:46.046 --> 00:02:51.646
It doesn't. spi up chooses images once,
and running it again keeps the lock unless

47
00:02:51.646 --> 00:02:53.176
you ask for a refresh.

48
00:02:53.646 --> 00:02:56.776
Only the shared environment
refreshes on a schedule.

49
00:02:57.086 --> 00:03:00.606
On yours, read the built date in spi info,

50
00:03:00.606 --> 00:03:03.736
and refresh before the mark
becomes a deleted image.

51
00:03:06.386 --> 00:03:07.516
So now you can say:

52
00:03:07.746 --> 00:03:12.316
each service runs its Azure
fork's main image from GHCR.

53
00:03:12.786 --> 00:03:17.036
The lock ties each tag to one
fork commit and one digest.

54
00:03:17.506 --> 00:03:21.756
And a canonical image ages toward
retention until a refresh moves it.

55
00:03:24.122 --> 00:03:27.492
Next: who does the stack think
you are, and what may you do?

